 title \"RECENT TRANSACTIONS\" and below the 3 rows a full-width secondary button \"Transaction History\" with Icon_History? Hmm, Icon_History is in header. Button \"Transaction History\" text-only is fine.\n\nActually, cleaner: header row with label left, and right side a small button \"Transaction History\". It's a bit long but fine with 13px font.\n\nCanvas may scroll vertically per Foundations — 03_Home may be taller than 844. I could make the frame 844 tall exactly (scrollable content). The brief says canvas 390×844, 03_Home may scroll vertically. I'll keep it 390×844 and fit content — need to fit a lot. Let me estimate: status bar 47, header ~48, balance card ~150, actions ~56+40, offer ~64, accounts grid 2×2 ~180, transactions ~3×64=192 + header 24, home indicator 34. Total ~800+. Tight but doable with compact spacing. Or make it taller (e.g., 1100) to allow scroll. The brief says \"may scroll\" — I'll keep 844 and arrange compactly. Let me plan precisely later during build.\n\n4. **04_Menu** — side menu over 03_Home with 03 partly visible. Structure: clone 03_Home content as background, dark scrim on right part, side menu panel (left, ~300 wide) with: avatar, \"Hallie Goebel\", email, menu items New Payment / Bank Accounts / All Transactions / Rewards & Offers / Settings (with icons), Logout at bottom (Icon_Logout). Prototype: tapping visible part of 03_Home → back to 03_Home.\n\n**Prototype links:**\n- 01: Get Started → 02 (as sheet overlay? \"sheet over 01_Onboarding\" — presentation is visual; prototype can be \"Navigate to\" or \"Open overlay\". I'll use Navigate to with Smart animate or slide-in. Since 02 contains the dimmed 01 background, simple navigate works visually. Actually nicer: use \"Open overlay\" from bottom? But then overlay must be separate frame. The brief says build exactly 4 frames and wire them. I'll use Navigate to with slide-up animation for 01→02 and 02→03, and 02 Cancel → 01. For 03 avatar → 04: Navigate to (04 contains dimmed 03 + menu). For 04 visible area → 03: Navigate to.\n\nHmm, but prototypes with overlays: I could instead make 02 a separate overlay frame... but brief says 4 named frames each containing full presentation (sheet over 01_Onboarding means the frame shows the sheet over onboarding). I'll do navigate-based with appropriate transitions.\n\n**Components to build as reusable (in workspace):** Since \"Component reuse\" is evaluated, I should create components in the workspace: e.g., \"Button/Primary\", \"Button/Secondary\", \"Text Field/Phone (focused)\", \"Header/Home\", \"Balance Card/Primary\", \"Account Tile\", \"Transaction Row\", \"Action Button\", \"Offer Banner\", \"Menu Row\", \"Side Menu\". But also reuse given components: Button (1:23), Text Field (1:26), Status Bar (1:33), Home Indicator (1:44), Numeric Keypad (1:46), and media components (illustration, avatars, banks, merchants, icons) as instances.\n\nGiven components live on page 1:3 — I can create instances of them in workspace page via `component.createInstance()` after getting node by id? Instance creation requires the component to be in the same document — yes, same file, different page is fine. `figma.getNodeByIdAsync('1:23')` then `.createInstance()`, then append to workspace page frame. Cross-page instance creation works in the Plugin API? Creating an instance of a component from another page is allowed (the instance just links to the component). Yes, `createInstance` works regardless of page; you then append the instance where you want.\n\nOne caveat: I must not modify the reference pages. Creating instances of components on those pages doesn't modify them. Good.\n\nCloning nodes from reference pages into workspace: cloning a node that lives on another page — `node.clone()` places the clone on the same page as the original I think. Actually clone() puts the clone as a sibling (same parent). To move to workspace page, append it after cloning. That would remove it from the reference page parent — appendChild