tion: totalW = w1+6+w2; x0=(390-totalW)/2. Sign in is \"Entry only (no frame)\" → no link.\n- Home indicator y=810.\n\nFonts: Inter Regular/Medium/Semi Bold/Bold. Load all four at start of every use_figma call that touches text (safe to reload).\n\n**Script structure (use_figma calls):**\n\nCall A (setup + components page scaffolding):\n- switch to workspace page (getNodeByIdAsync('6:2') → PageNode, setCurrentPageAsync).\n- load fonts.\n- create a \"Design System / Components\" container? I'll create components inline during builds where needed (createComponent, add children, then instance). Plan local components:\n  - `Home/Account Tile` → 4 instances\n  - `Home/Transaction Row` → 3 instances\n  - `Menu/Row` → 6 instances (5 + logout w/ overrides)\n  - `UI/Icon Button` (history, refresh?) maybe skip\n  Creating components adds complexity (instances + overrides). Given grading includes component reuse, worth it. But each component adds code. Let me limit to: AccountTile, TransactionRow, MenuRow. Plus reuse of given components. Also the balance card, banner, header as components? They're single-use; components for single-use still count as \"reusable components built\" (brief says design them as reusable components). But time/call budget… I'll componentize: Header/Home, BalanceCard, ActionButton (used 3×: Send, Receive, Scan w/ overrides), OfferBanner, AccountTile(×4), TransactionRow(×3), MenuRow(×6), Field/Phone, Sheet maybe not. That's a solid component story.\n\nHmm, this significantly increases code. Risk management: build components in Call A/B, screens in C/D/E, prototypes in F. If something fails, iterate.\n\nActually, let me simplify: components placed in a \"Components\" frame on workspace page; screens instantiate them. To override text inside instances: find text nodes by name. I'll design components with named children (e.g., \"Label\", \"Value\", \"IconSlot\").\n\nGiven complexity, maybe balance: componentize the repeated ones (AccountTile ×4, TransactionRow ×3, MenuRow ×6, ActionButton ×3) — real reuse — and build single-use blocks (header, card, banner, sheet, menu container) directly in frames. The brief technically says design those as reusable components too, but reuse criterion is best evidenced by actual multi-instance usage. I'll ALSO wrap single-use blocks as components if budget allows (Header, BalanceCard, OfferBanner). Let me try to include them — create component, then instance into screen, keeps screens clean.\n\nWait, there's a subtlety: components must be created with figma.createComponent() at page root or inside a frame, then createInstance. ComponentNode itself lives on the canvas (I'll keep them in a \"Local Components\" frame at, say, x=0, y=1100 area of workspace — away from screens). Instances go into screens.\n\nMedia instances inside my components: e.g., AccountTile component contains an instance of Bank_Axis; for other instances (HDFC etc.) I'd need to swap the nested instance — instance swap of nested instances: allowed via `swapComponent` on the nested instance within each parent instance. Yes, instance children can be swapped. So: AccountTile has nested inst of Bank_Axis named \"BankMark\"; per instance: `inst.findOne(n=>n.name=='BankMark').swapComponent(hdfcNode)`, override texts. \n\nTransactionRow: nested icon container — three different icon types (merchant paytm 72², avatar risa, merchant amazon 26²). Swap + resize. Doable.\n\nMenuRow: nested Icon instance swap + label override + color override for Logout.\n\nOK. Also avatars: Avatar_Hallie in header (40) and menu (56) — instances resized.\n\n**Inspecting given components:** In Call A, I'll log structures of Button (1:23), Text Field (1:26), Status Bar (1:33), Home Indicator (1:44), Keypad (1:46) — names/types/sizes/colors of children — returned in tool output, informing Call B overrides. This costs one call but saves guesswork. I'll include in Call A: create nothing yet except switching page + inspection + font load test. Actually